LeetCode 2130 — Maximum Twin Sum of a Linked List
Problem Understanding

You are given a singly linked list of even length.

For every node at index i, its twin is the node at index:

$$ n-1-i $$

So we need:

Maximum Twin Sum = max(left node value + corresponding right node value)

Example:

1 → 2 → 3 → 4
↑           ↑
twin pair: 1 + 4 = 5

    2 → 3
    twin pair: 2 + 3 = 5

Answer = 5

We can solve this from brute force → optimal.

Approach 1 — Convert Linked List to ArrayList
Idea

Since linked lists don't support random access efficiently, first copy all values into an ArrayList.

Then use two pointers:

i → beginning
j → end

Calculate:

al[i] + al[j]

and move both toward the center.

Code

class Solution {
    public int pairSum(ListNode head) {
        ArrayList<Integer> al = new ArrayList<>();

        while (head != null) {
            al.add(head.val);
            head = head.next;
        }

        int i = 0;
        int j = al.size() - 1;
        int ans = 0;

        while (i <= j) {
            ans = Math.max(ans, al.get(i) + al.get(j));
            i++;
            j--;
        }

        return ans;
    }
}

Dry Run

Consider:

5 → 4 → 2 → 1

Array:

[5, 4, 2, 1]
i	j	Pair	Sum	ans
0	3	5 + 1	6	6
1	2	4 + 2	6	6

Answer:

6
Complexity
Time: O(n)
Space: O(n)

The extra space is needed for the array.

Approach 2 — Stack
Idea

A stack gives us access to elements in reverse order.

We can:

Traverse the linked list and push every value into a stack.
Traverse the linked list again.
For each node, pop its twin from the stack.
Calculate the sum.

Because the stack contains:

top → last element
       second last
       ...
       first element

it naturally gives us the twin from the opposite side.

Code

class Solution {
    public int pairSum(ListNode head) {
        Stack<Integer> st = new Stack<>();

        ListNode curr = head;

        while (curr != null) {
            st.push(curr.val);
            curr = curr.next;
        }

        int ans = 0;
        curr = head;

        while (curr != null) {
            ans = Math.max(ans, curr.val + st.pop());
            curr = curr.next;
        }

        return ans;
    }
}

Dry Run
1 → 2 → 3 → 4

After pushing:

Stack:

4 ← top
3
2
1

Traverse the linked list again:

Current	Stack Pop	Sum	ans
1	4	5	5
2	3	5	5
3	2	5	5
4	1	5	5

Answer:

5

We technically calculate every pair twice. We could stop after n/2 nodes, but the complexity remains the same.

Complexity
Time: O(n)
Space: O(n)

Approach 3 — Reverse the Second Half ⭐

This is the optimal approach.

Core Idea

We don't actually need an array or stack.

We can manipulate the linked list itself.

Use three steps:

1. Find middle
2. Reverse second half
3. Compare first half with reversed second half

For:

1 → 2 → 3 → 4

Find middle:

1 → 2 → 3 → 4
        ↑
       slow

Reverse from slow:

1 → 2       4 → 3

Now compare:

1 + 4 = 5
2 + 3 = 5

Answer:
5

Your Optimal Code

class Solution {
    public int pairSum(ListNode head) {
        ListNode slow = head;
        ListNode fast = head;

        // Find the beginning of the second half
        while (fast != null && fast.next != null) {
            slow = slow.next;
            fast = fast.next.next;
        }

        // Reverse the second half
        ListNode prev = null;

        while (slow != null) {
            ListNode next = slow.next;
            slow.next = prev;
            prev = slow;
            slow = next;
        }

        // Compare both halves
        int ans = 0;

        while (prev != null) {
            ans = Math.max(ans, head.val + prev.val);
            head = head.next;
            prev = prev.next;
        }

        return ans;
    }
}

